```
ROLE: product_realist  VOTE: yellow
REAL_USE_CASE: Eval harness maintainers and researchers running self-consistency (maj@k) on free-form LLM outputs (math, code, QA) where answer surface forms vary. Specifically when comparing models whose correct-vs-error fragmentation asymmetry (phi) differs—e.g., a strong model with concentrated correct answers and fragmented errors vs. a weaker model with fragmented correct answers and a dominant wrong attractor.
OPERATIONAL_SIGNIFICANCE: Microbenchmark-only. L0 demonstrates a 30-point accuracy swing (0.985→0.688 at phi=-0.9, z=-19.8; 0.111→0.686 at phi=+0.9, z=+41.6) and a constructed ranking inversion (B>A at r=0, A>B at r=1, |z|>48) under synthetic generative model (N=60, K=20, 30 seeds, p_C∈[0.28,0.50], phi∈±0.9). Effect persists at full precision (q=1.0, z=-28.2) confirming pure-recall phenomenon, but attenuates to null at q=0.6 (z=-0.4). No real-model, real-benchmark, or real-canonicalizer (exact-match vs Minerva/sympy) validation provided. Ecological plausibility of phi extremes and recall gap sufficient to flip published leaderboards remains unproven—explicitly flagged as open question in packet ("Is the harness-induced ranking inversion ECOLOGICALLY plausible?").
ADOPTION_BARRIER: [1] No method to estimate phi from unlabeled outputs (L1 item 2 unaddressed)—operators cannot know a priori whether higher recall helps or hurts. [2] Leaderboard lock-in: existing benchmarks (HELM, OpenLLM, etc.) fix canonicalizer; changing breaks comparability and requires re-running historical models. [3] Per-model tuning burden: optimal recall depends on model-specific fragmentation, increasing harness complexity vs. one-size-fits-all. [4] Risk of hypothetical problem: no cited instance of a real published ranking actually flipping due to canonicalizer recall; solving synthetic inversion may not translate to production impact.
RATIONALE: Packet L0 RESULTS (EXP-0050) is synthetic-only: "CPU-only, stdlib-only, SERIAL. GT harness-owned; canonicalizer never reads GT class" with generative parameters M_INC=3, DOM_FRAC=0.7, SC=SW=6. Ranking inversion uses constructed params "a_pc[0.45,0.65] x b_pc[0.20,0.40]" (packet), not observed on real checkpoints. Precision-control shows "effect ATTENUATES as precision drops... ~gone @q0.6 z=-0.4" (packet), limiting scope to high-precision regime—realistic for target canonicalizers but unvalidated. Packet explicitly asks: "Is the harness-induced ranking inversion ECOLOGICALLY plausible — do real eval graders differ enough in equivalence recall (lm-eval-harness exact-match vs Minerva/sympy-normalized) to actually flip a published leaderboard?"—insufficient evidence to answer yes. Clean controlled result merits YELLOW (proceed to L1 real-data validation), not GREEN.
```
